    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hi, i'm looking for some help with a tax question. my family is dealing with an estate that has properties in both the uk and spain, plus some offshore accounts. i need to understand how inheritance tax works when assets are spread out like this and what the main residency rules are for the beneficiaries. thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Inheritance tax (Inheritance Tax, or Inheritance Tax IHT) in the UK does not generally arise until assets exceed the nil rate band, set at £325,000 in 2021, though this can vary depending on certain reliefs including those for overseas assets. A lump sum of up to £20,000 from the worldwide estate may also fall outside IHT.\n\nAs for the beneficiaries, the main residency rule for Inheritance Tax purposes is that if a beneficiary is not a UK resident, they may be able to use the overseas nil rate band granted under the Finance Act 2006. This places more favorable limits on estate value for overseas estate as compared to IHT in the UK, but each country's system is slightly different, and transfer tax responsibilities may still apply in the property-owning country.\n\nConsulting a specialized tax professional with international estate planning experience will ensure that you understand all obligations and Make most of available exemptions and reliefs.",
      "source": "target",
